\begin_layout Standard
When a switching impulse is applied to a long air gap with a strongly non-uniform field, such as a rod-plane gap, the discharge does not begin with breakdown. Its first stage is the \series bold
impulse corona
\series default
: bursts of branched streamers that start from the rod tip once the field there is high enough. Every burst leaves positive space charge in the gap. This charge lowers the field at the tip and stops the discharge for a while, the dark period, until it has drifted away and the voltage has risen further; then the next burst follows. The number, timing and size of these bursts decide when the next stage, the leader, can start, and with it the breakdown behaviour of the gap 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lesrenardieres1977"
literal "false"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This thesis develops a software application that \series bold
estimates the development of the impulse corona discharge
\series default
 in a rod-plane gap. It follows the model of Wang and Wang 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
: the space charge of every burst is a cone of charge in front of the tip. Its field and the fields of its images in the rod and in the plane are computed by numerical integration, the applied field by the Charge Simulation Method, and the drift of the charge between bursts from the ion mobility. For a given impulse the program finds when each burst occurs, the length and charge of its streamers, how the space charge moves, and the total electric field at any point and time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The thesis has two aims: to derive and implement the model in a general form, usable for any rod-plane arrangement once its inputs have been measured in the laboratory; and to test it against the results published in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Chapter 1 states the problem and the assumptions. Chapter 2 derives the space-charge field components and the charge of the space-charge cloud, and Chapter 3 shows that they are equivalent to the published formulation. Chapter 4 adds the time dependence: the impulse voltage, the drift of the space charge and the successive corona bursts; Chapter 5 collects the complete model. Chapters 6 and 7 compare its results with Figures 3 to 8 of the paper. The appendices describe the Charge Simulation Method, the impulse parameters and how the inputs are measured, and they give the parameters that had to be retrieved from the paper's figures.
\end_layout

