\begin_layout Subsection
The Impulse Voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:impulse_voltage"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the gap is stressed by a switching impulse, the applied voltage changes with time. The impulse is modelled as a double exponential,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V(t)=V_{p}\,k\left(e^{-\alpha t}-e^{-\beta t}\right),\label{eq:impulse_voltage}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}$
\end_inset
 is the crest voltage. In high-voltage engineering an impulse is described by its crest voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}$
\end_inset
, its time to crest 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{cr}$
\end_inset
 (the “time of wavefront” of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
) and its time to half value 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}$
\end_inset
, the instant on the tail where the voltage has fallen to 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}/2$
\end_inset
, as defined in IEC 60060-1 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "iec60060"
literal "false"

\end_inset
. The constants 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 follow from these three quantities; how they are found is shown in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:impulse_fit"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout


\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Applied Field During the Impulse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The applied field is the Laplacian field of the electrodes, so it is proportional to the applied voltage. If 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ex}(z)$
\end_inset
 is the applied field for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\,\text{V}$
\end_inset
 on the rod, then at any instant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{ex}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z).\label{eq:applied_field_linear}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Charge Simulation Method (Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:csm"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
) is therefore solved only once, for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\,\text{V}$
\end_inset
, and its result is scaled by 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
 at every time step. The space charge fields do not depend on the voltage directly: they change only through the drift, whose speed depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}(z_{c},t)$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

