\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Successive-Burst Model 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:bursts"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So far the model follows one cloud, the space charge of the first corona streamer. During an impulse the rod emits several streamers (bursts), one after the other, and the space charge grows with every burst. This chapter extends the model to any number of bursts. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:burst_geometry"
plural "false"
caps "true"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 shows the clouds at the instant the third burst occurs: the two clouds of the earlier bursts have already drifted, and a new one, the third, has just formed at the tip; and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:burst_timeline"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 shows how the bursts follow each other in time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Two Time Scales
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A streamer of length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
 grows at the streamer velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{s}$
\end_inset
, in the time 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{s}=L_{cs}/v_{s}$
\end_inset
, while the ions drift at 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{d}=\mu^{+}E$
\end_inset
. Because 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{s}\gg v_{d}$
\end_inset
, the ions hardly move while a streamer grows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{d}\,\tau_{s}=\frac{\mu^{+}E}{v_{s}}\,L_{cs}\ll L_{cs}.\label{eq:time_scales}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A burst is therefore modelled as instantaneous: its cone appears at once, with its apex at the rod tip, and only afterwards drifts. The real growth of the space charge, a cone that keeps its apex at the tip and becomes larger, is represented by a sequence of such cones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Clouds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Burst 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,2,\dots$
\end_inset
 happens at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}$
\end_inset
 and leaves a cloud with its own length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
, the common cone angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 and the common shape constants 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
. Its charge follows from Gauss's law over the head of its cone, 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:gauss_charge"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, minus the charge of the older clouds that still lies inside the new cone, because the flux through the head counts every charge inside the cone; its normalisation constant follows from the charge as in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:amplitude_A"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
. Both depend on the length of the new cloud, which is not known yet, so they are written for a trial length 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{k}(L)=\pi\epsilon_{0}E_{c}L^{2}\tan^{2}\left(\theta_{B}/2\right)-\sum_{j<k}Q_{j}^{in}(L,t_{k}),\qquad A_{k}(L)=\frac{Q_{k}(L)}{2\pi\left[1-\cos\left(\theta_{B}/2\right)\right]\int_{0}^{L}\frac{r^{2}}{(r+C)^{B}}\,dr}.\label{eq:burst_cloud}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because Gauss's law ties the charge to the length, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{k}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}$
\end_inset
 are not further unknowns. The actual length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
 follows from the head condition of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:growth"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
; then 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{k}=Q_{k}(L_{k})$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}=A_{k}(L_{k})$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{j}^{in}(L,t)$
\end_inset
 is the charge of the older cloud 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset
, drifted by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,j}(t)$
\end_inset
, that lies inside the new cloud of length 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
. It is the volume integral of the density, 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:charge_volume"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, applied to the older cloud (with its own 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{j}$
\end_inset
) but with a different upper limit for 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{j}^{in}(L,t)=2\pi A_{j}\int_{0}^{\theta_{B}/2}\int_{0}^{r_{in}(\theta)}\frac{r^{2}\sin\theta}{(r+C)^{B}}\,dr\,d\theta,\quad r_{in}(\theta)=\min\left[L_{j},\;-z_{d,j}\cos\theta+\sqrt{L^{2}-z_{d,j}^{2}\sin^{2}\theta}\right].\label{eq:burst_enclosed}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/enclosed_charge.pdf
	width 100col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Charge of an older cloud 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset
 inside the new cloud 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 (schematic, not to scale). Both cones share the axis and the angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
; the older one has drifted by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,j}$
\end_inset
. Along each direction 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset
 from its apex 
\begin_inset Formula $O'$
\end_inset
 the integration runs up to 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{in}(\theta)$
\end_inset
: (a) up to the head of the new cloud, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\overline{OP}\right|=L_{k}$
\end_inset
; (b) up to the end of the older cloud, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{j}$
\end_inset
, when it lies entirely inside.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:enclosed_charge"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The upper limit follows from the geometry of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:enclosed_charge"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
. Both cones share the axis and the angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
, and the older one has only moved forward along the axis by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,j}$
\end_inset
. Seen from the tip 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset
, every point of it is therefore within the opening angle of the new cone, so the angular limit stays 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}/2$
\end_inset
, and a point belongs to the new cloud when its distance from 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset
 is at most 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
. For a point 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset
 at distance 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset
 from the older apex 
\begin_inset Formula $O'$
\end_inset
, in the direction 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset
, the triangle 
\begin_inset Formula $OO'P$
\end_inset
 gives, by the law of cosines,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|\overline{OP}\right|^{2}=z_{d,j}^{2}+r^{2}+2\,z_{d,j}\,r\cos\theta\le L^{2}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r\le-z_{d,j}\cos\theta+\sqrt{L^{2}-z_{d,j}^{2}\sin^{2}\theta}.\label{eq:enclosed_geometry}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Along each direction the integration therefore runs from the apex of the older cloud up to the head of the new one, or up to the end of the older cloud if that comes first, hence the minimum in 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{in}(\theta)$
\end_inset
. The part of the older cloud beyond the head (dashed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:enclosed_charge"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
a) lies outside the Gaussian surface of the new cloud and is not subtracted; an older cloud that lies entirely inside (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:enclosed_charge"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
b) is subtracted whole, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{j}^{in}=Q_{j}$
\end_inset
. The less an older cloud has drifted (
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,j}\ll L$
\end_inset
), the larger the part of it that is subtracted.
\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
For the first burst the sum is empty. If an older cloud has drifted out of the new cone, nothing is subtracted for it. Each cloud then drifts as a rigid body by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,k}(t)$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,k}(t)=0$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\le t_{k}$
\end_inset
. Its field is the sum of the three space-charge fields of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:drift_image_fields"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, evaluated with 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,k}(t)$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{k}(z,t)=E_{sp,k}(z,t)+E_{I_{rod},k}(z,t)+E_{I_{plane},k}(z,t).\label{eq:cloud_field}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Total Space Charge and the Recursive Total Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/burst_geometry.pdf
	width 95col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The burst model at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{3}$
\end_inset
 of the third burst. The new cloud (solid) has its apex at the rod tip and the length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{3}$
\end_inset
 at which the field at its head is 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
. The history clouds (dashed) have drifted by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,1}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,2}$
\end_inset
; each moves with the velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{v}_{k}$
\end_inset
 at its own point 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c,k}$
\end_inset
. Right: the part of a cloud beyond the plane is absorbed, so its radial limit becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{max}$
\end_inset
.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:burst_geometry"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The space charge in the gap is the sum of every cloud created so far, each shifted by its own drift (
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{k}(\vec{x})$
\end_inset
 is the density of cloud 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 with its apex at the rod tip):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho(\vec{x},t)=\sum_{k:\,t_{k}\le t}\rho_{k}\left(\vec{x}-z_{d,k}(t)\,\hat{z}\right),\qquad Q(t)=\sum_{k:\,t_{k}\le t}Q_{k}.\label{eq:total_density}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fields are linear in the charge, so the total field is the applied field plus the field of every cloud:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{total}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z)+\sum_{k:\,t_{k}\le t}E_{k}(z,t).\label{eq:total_field_bursts}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For burst 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
, the clouds of the earlier bursts form its 
\series bold
history
\series default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{k}(z,t)=\sum_{j<k}E_{j}(z,t),\qquad H_{1}(z,t)=0,\label{eq:history}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which grows by one cloud at every burst,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{k+1}(z,t)=H_{k}(z,t)+E_{k}(z,t).\label{eq:history_recursion}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Between the bursts 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k+1$
\end_inset
 the total field is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{total}=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z)+H_{k}(z,t)+E_{k}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z)+H_{k+1}(z,t)$
\end_inset
: every new burst is computed on top of the drifting history of the earlier ones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Burst Condition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/burst_timeline.pdf
	width 95col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The recursion in time (schematic). The tip field rises with 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
 until it crosses the threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}^{*}$
\end_inset
; after the time lag the burst follows at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}$
\end_inset
, when the tip field reaches the field required for burst 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{req,k}=E_{0}+k_{lag}\,\dot{E}_{tip}(t_{k}^{*})^{n}$
\end_inset
 (drawn at one level for simplicity; it is higher on a faster rise). At each burst a cloud is added to the history and the tip field drops; it then recovers because the voltage rises and the clouds drift away.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:burst_timeline"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A streamer can start only when the field at the tip is high enough, but it does not start at the very instant this happens: a free electron must first appear in the high-field region, and its avalanche must grow into a streamer. During this 
\series bold
time lag
\series default
 the voltage keeps rising, so on a fast front the streamer starts at a higher field than on a slow one. Burst 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 therefore has two instants: the tip field first reaches the threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset
,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}t_{k}^{*}=\min\left\{ t>t_{k-1}\,:\;E_{tip}(t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(0)+H_{k}(0,t)=E_{0}\right\} ,\qquad E_{0}=V_{0}\,e_{ex}(0),\label{eq:burst_threshold}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the burst follows when the tip field has risen further, by an amount that grows with how fast it was rising at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}^{*}$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t_{k}=\min\left\{ t>t_{k}^{*}\,:\;E_{tip}(t)=E_{0}+k_{lag}\left(\left.\frac{dE_{tip}}{dt}\right|_{t_{k}^{*}}\right)^{n}\right\} \label{eq:burst_condition}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
 is the static inception voltage, below which no streamer can start, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}=V_{0}\,e_{ex}(0)$
\end_inset
 is the corresponding field at the tip; 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{lag}$
\end_inset
 sets the size of the overvoltage and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 how fast it grows with the rate of rise. For the first burst (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}=0$
\end_inset
) the law reads 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}=V_{0}+k\,(dV/dt)^{n}$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $k=k_{lag}\,e_{ex}(0)^{\,n-1}$
\end_inset
. The law, the criterion it comes from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kind1957,darveniza1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
 and how the rate 
\begin_inset Formula $dE_{tip}/dt$
\end_inset
 is evaluated during the simulation are given in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:fw_time_lag"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
; how 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 are measured, in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:app_inputs"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout


\begin_layout Subsection
Growth of the New Cloud
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:growth"

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During the burst the older clouds stay where they are, at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,j}(t_{k})$
\end_inset
, because of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:time_scales"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
. The streamer keeps growing while the field at its head is above 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
. Its length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
 is the length beyond which the head field stays below 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
, the 
\series bold
largest
\series default
 root of the head condition:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L_{k}=\max\left\{ L\,:\;F_{k}(L)=V(t_{k})\,e_{ex}(L)+A_{k}(L)\,g_{k}(L)+H_{k}(L,t_{k})-E_{c}=0\right\},\label{eq:burst_length}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{k}(L)$
\end_inset
 is the field at the head of the new, not yet drifted cone of length 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $A=1$
\end_inset
 (the field is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
), and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}(L)$
\end_inset
 is the normalisation constant of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_cloud"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 for that length. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{k}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}$
\end_inset
 follow from 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
, the head condition is one equation for the single unknown 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
. It is solved as follows (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:length_flow"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For a trial length 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
, compute 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{k}(L)$
\end_inset
 from Gauss's law minus the older charge inside, and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}(L)$
\end_inset
 from it, 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_cloud"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Evaluate the head condition 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{k}(L)$
\end_inset
, with the field of the new cloud 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}(L)\,g_{k}(L)$
\end_inset
 and the frozen history 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{k}(L,t_{k})$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Scan 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
 outward from the tip and refine the last change of sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{k}$
\end_inset
 by root finding: this is 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
With 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
 known, fix 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{k}=Q_{k}(L_{k})$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}=A_{k}(L_{k})$
\end_inset
 and the reference point 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c,k,0}$
\end_inset
 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_zc"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/length_flow.pdf
	width 80col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
How the length, the charge and the normalisation constant of a new cloud are found together. For every trial length the charge follows from Gauss's law and the normalisation constant from the charge, so the head condition 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{k}(L)=0$
\end_inset
 has the length as its only unknown.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:length_flow"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
The largest root, and not the first one, is used because of the history. On slow fronts the drifted apex of an older cloud, its densest part (
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{j}$
\end_inset
 is largest at 
\begin_inset Formula $r=0$
\end_inset
), lowers the field in a narrow region in front of it, and the head field can dip below 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
 there. The streamer is assumed to cross such a narrow dip: it is exaggerated by the rigid drift, which keeps the apex of the cloud sharp, while a real cloud spreads out. The width of every crossed dip is recorded; in the simulations of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:repro_fig678"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 none occurs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the first burst (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}=0$
\end_inset
) there is only one root. The cone angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 is measured (Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:app_inputs"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
); with it, every length, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{1}$
\end_inset
 included, follows from the head condition.
\end_layout


\begin_layout Subsection
Drift of Many Clouds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each cloud moves with the ion velocity at its own reference point 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c,k}$
\end_inset
, driven by every field except its own direct field:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{sp,k}(z_{c,k,0},t_{k})=0,\qquad z_{c,k}(t)=z_{c,k,0}+z_{d,k}(t),\label{eq:burst_zc}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{dz_{d,k}}{dt}=\mu^{+}\Big[V(t)\,e_{ex}(z_{c,k})+E_{I_{rod},k}(z_{c,k},t)+E_{I_{plane},k}(z_{c,k},t)+\sum_{j\neq k}E_{j}(z_{c,k},t)\Big],\qquad z_{d,k}(t_{k})=0.\label{eq:burst_drift}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The clouds are coupled: each one is pushed by the fields of the others. The drifts are therefore one system of ordinary differential equations, integrated together, which gains one equation at every burst. Clouds may overlap; by 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:total_density"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 their densities simply add.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Absorption by the Plane
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A charge element of cloud 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 sits at 
\begin_inset Formula $r\cos(\theta)+z_{d,k}(t)$
\end_inset
. It reaches the grounded plane and is neutralized when
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r\cos(\theta)+z_{d,k}(t)\ge d.\label{eq:absorption_element}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The field integrals then run only over the charge that is still in the gap. The upper radial limit becomes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{max,k}(\theta,t)=\min\left[L_{k},\;\frac{d-z_{d,k}(t)}{\cos(\theta)}\right],\label{eq:absorption_limit}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
so the charge of the cloud decreases:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{k}(t)=2\pi\int_{0}^{\theta_{B}/2}\int_{0}^{r_{max,k}(\theta,t)}\rho_{k}(r)\,r^{2}\sin(\theta)\,dr\,d\theta.\label{eq:absorption_charge}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The cloud is completely absorbed, and removed, when its apex reaches the plane:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{d,k}(t)\ge d.\label{eq:absorption_cloud}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With an average field of about 
\begin_inset Formula $V/d$
\end_inset
 in the gap, the time an ion needs to cross it is of the order
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t_{cross}\approx\frac{d}{\mu^{+}\,V/d}=\frac{d^{2}}{\mu^{+}V}.\label{eq:crossing_time}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{cross}$
\end_inset
 is much longer than the impulse, no cloud reaches the plane while the voltage is applied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
End of the Simulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The simulation covers a time window 
\begin_inset Formula $0\le t\le t_{end}$
\end_inset
. Here the bursts are searched on the front of the impulse, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{end}=T_{cr}$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
after the crest 
\begin_inset Formula $dV/dt<0$
\end_inset
, so the applied field at the tip falls and a new burst can only come from the clouds drifting away;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
in long gaps the streamers on the front lead to the inception of a leader, which the model does not include. The model describes the corona bursts before the leader.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The First Two Bursts, Step by Step 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:two_bursts"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The recursive equations are compact, but it may help to follow them through the first two bursts, to see which terms appear and when. Every new burst adds one cloud, and with it one term to every sum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\series bold
Before the first burst.
\series default
 No space charge: the field is the applied field alone,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{total}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z),\qquad E_{tip}(t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(0).
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\series bold
Burst 1.
\series default
 The tip field reaches the threshold at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t_{1}^{*})\,e_{ex}(0)=E_{0}$
\end_inset
; the burst follows at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}$
\end_inset
, when
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V(t_{1})\,e_{ex}(0)=E_{0}+k_{lag}\left(\dot{V}(t_{1}^{*})\,e_{ex}(0)\right)^{n},\qquad V_{1}=V(t_{1}).\label{eq:burst1_inception}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Its charge and normalisation constant depend on its length (Gauss's law alone: there is no older cloud to subtract), and the head condition, with only its own field, fixes that length:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}Q_{1}(L)=\pi\epsilon_{0}E_{c}L^{2}\tan^{2}\left(\theta_{B}/2\right),\qquad A_{1}(L)=A\left(Q_{1}(L),L\right),\\ V_{1}\,e_{ex}(L_{1})+A_{1}(L_{1})\,g_{1}(L_{1})=E_{c}\;\Rightarrow\;L_{1},\qquad Q_{1}=Q_{1}(L_{1}),\quad A_{1}=A_{1}(L_{1}).\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
\series bold
Between bursts 1 and 2.
\series default
 One cloud drifts, pushed by the applied field and its own images; the total field has one space-charge term:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{dz_{d,1}}{dt}=\mu^{+}\Big[V(t)\,e_{ex}(z_{c,1})+E_{I_{rod},1}(z_{c,1})+E_{I_{plane},1}(z_{c,1})\Big],\qquad E_{total}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z)+E_{1}(z,t).
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Right after the burst the cloud lowers the tip field below the threshold, since 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{1}(0,t_{1})<0$
\end_inset
. As the cloud drifts away and the voltage keeps rising, the tip field recovers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\series bold
Burst 2.
\series default
 The tip field, now with the field of cloud 1, reaches the threshold again at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset
; its rate of rise now includes the drift of cloud 1, and the burst follows at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{tip}(t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(0)+E_{1}(0,t),\qquad E_{tip}(t_{2}^{*})=E_{0},\qquad E_{tip}(t_{2})=E_{0}+k_{lag}\left(\dot{E}_{tip}(t_{2}^{*})\right)^{n}.
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The head condition now contains the frozen field of cloud 1 as history, 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{2}=E_{1}$
\end_inset
, and the Gauss charge of the new cloud loses the part of cloud 1 that lies inside the new cone:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}Q_{2}(L)=\pi\epsilon_{0}E_{c}L^{2}\tan^{2}\left(\theta_{B}/2\right)-Q_{1}^{in}(L,t_{2}),\qquad A_{2}(L)=A\left(Q_{2}(L),L\right),\\ V(t_{2})\,e_{ex}(L_{2})+A_{2}(L_{2})\,g_{2}(L_{2})+E_{1}(L_{2},t_{2})=E_{c}\;(\text{largest root})\\ \Rightarrow\;L_{2},\qquad Q_{2}=Q_{2}(L_{2}),\quad A_{2}=A_{2}(L_{2}).\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
\series bold
After burst 2.
\series default
 Two clouds drift, and each is pushed also by the full field of the other:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}\frac{dz_{d,1}}{dt} & =\mu^{+}\Big[V\,e_{ex}(z_{c,1})+E_{I_{rod},1}+E_{I_{plane},1}+E_{2}(z_{c,1})\Big],\\\frac{dz_{d,2}}{dt} & =\mu^{+}\Big[V\,e_{ex}(z_{c,2})+E_{I_{rod},2}+E_{I_{plane},2}+E_{1}(z_{c,2})\Big],\end{aligned}\qquad E_{total}=V\,e_{ex}+E_{1}+E_{2}.
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\series bold
And so on.
\series default
 Burst 3 sees the history 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{3}=E_{1}+E_{2}$
\end_inset
, subtracts 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{1}^{in}+Q_{2}^{in}$
\end_inset
 from its Gauss charge, and adds a third drift equation and a third term 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{3}$
\end_inset
 to the total field; each cloud's drift equation gains the field of the new cloud. A cloud that reaches the plane is removed, and its terms disappear from every sum.
\end_layout

