\begin_layout Subsection
Comparing Our Curves with the Paper's 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:repro_fitting"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 3 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
 gives two curves, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset
, but not all the parameters that produced them (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:repro_fig3_given"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). The missing parameters are found by fitting: they are chosen so that our curves come as close as possible to the paper's. This section explains, without the mathematics, how “as close as possible” is measured and how it is searched.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a given set of parameters we compute our curves and, at every point digitized from the paper, measure the gap between our value and the paper's. Each gap is squared, so that gaps above and below the paper's curve do not cancel and one large miss counts more than several small ones, and the squares are added into a single number, the error. The 
\series bold
least-squares fit
\series default
 is the set of parameters with the smallest error.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two details make the comparison fair. The paper's curves reach about −50 kV/cm near the tip but only about 5 kV/cm near the head of the streamer, so the gaps of each curve are divided by the typical size of that curve; otherwise the steep part near the tip would decide everything. Each curve is also divided by its number of points, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset
 count equally. Points closer than 
\begin_inset Formula $0.1\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 to the apex are left out, because there the curves are too steep to be digitized reliably. The error is therefore relative: an error of 0.37 means that our curves miss the paper's by about 37% of their typical size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The charge does not have to be searched. All the fields are proportional to it, so for any shape of the cone the best charge follows directly from a simple formula, and only the shape parameters are searched. We first compute the error on a coarse grid of values, which shows the whole landscape and whether one clear best fit exists, and then let an optimizer refine the best grid points. If even the best fit leaves a large error, no choice of these parameters can reproduce the paper's curves: the difference then lies in the model, in the way the paper computed its curves, or in the data, but not in the parameter values.
\end_layout

