\begin_layout Subsubsection
What the Paper Gives 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:repro_fig3_given"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For Figure 3 the paper gives a rod-plane gap of 
\begin_inset Formula $d=80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, a tip radius 
\begin_inset Formula $R=0.1\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, a first-streamer inception voltage of 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 and a streamer length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}=2.7\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. About the charge it says only that the field in the streamer region is about 
\begin_inset Formula $5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 and nearly uniform, and that “the constant A is chosen to satisfy (a); B and C are chosen to meet (b). Usually, B is 1.5 and C is about 0.035” 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
. It does not give the cone angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
, the normalisation constant 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, or the unit of 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
. These are found by fitting (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:repro_fitting"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). The fields are computed with the equations of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:equivalence"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, which are those of the paper.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Fitting Cases 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:repro_fig3_cases"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset
 were fitted to the digitized curves under six different assumptions (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_fig3_cases"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). The first four test possible explanations for the difference between our curves and the paper's; the last two are the best fits our model can give:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Case 1, r1 typo: 
\series default
 the paper prints the distance 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset
 of its Eq. (4) with 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{3}\sin\theta$
\end_inset
 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $r\sin\theta$
\end_inset
. We computed 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 with the printed expression, in case it was also in the authors' program; B and C as in the paper.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Case 2, coarse quadrature: 
\series default
 the paper evaluates the integrals by Gaussian quadrature without saying how many points it used. We used only 4 × 4 points, in case a coarse rule shaped the paper's curves; 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
, B and C fitted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Case 3, paper's B and C: 
\series default 
\begin_inset Formula $B=1.5$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C=0.035\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 as given; only 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 fitted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Case 4, C in metres: 
\series default
 the paper gives no unit for C; here 
\begin_inset Formula $C=0.035\,\text{m}=3.5\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $B=1.5$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Case 5, full fit: 
\series default 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
, B and C all fitted, with 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}=2.7\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 as given.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Case 6, free 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
:
\series default
 as case 5, with the streamer length also fitted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In every case the charge is fitted as well (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:repro_fitting"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
