\begin_layout Subsubsection
Reading the Results 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:repro_fig3_reading"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Rejected explanations.
\series default
 The r1 typo and the coarse quadrature fit clearly worse than the paper's own B and C with our equations (errors 0.71 and 0.66 against 0.44), and C in metres fits no better (0.44). None of them explains the paper's curves.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
What agrees.
\series default
 In every case our curves have the paper's shape: 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 is strongly negative at the tip, changes sign about half a centimetre in front of it and stays positive along the streamer, while 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset
 is negative and confined close to the rod.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
What differs.
\series default
 Even the full fit leaves an error of 0.37. The difference is systematic: a charge large enough to give the paper's strong negative fields near the tip makes our 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 along the streamer about twice the paper's (at 
\begin_inset Formula $z=2.3\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $10.5$
\end_inset
 against 
\begin_inset Formula $5.3\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 in case 5), and a charge that matches along the streamer is too weak near the tip.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Case 6.
\series default
 It has the smallest error, but its streamer length runs to the upper limit of the search, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}=3.5\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, and its 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 keeps rising beyond the end of the paper's data. It fits better only by moving the charge out of the compared range, not by reproducing the paper's shape; a shorter charge region fits worse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
The parameters are not pinned down.
\series default
 Around the full fit the error changes little: cone angles between 110° and 160° and exponents B between 1.0 and 1.8 give errors within about 10% of the best (Map sheets of the full-fit workbook).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The position of the maximum can be explained without any calculation. In the model the charge ends at the head of the cone. Moving along the axis towards the head, more and more of the charge lies behind the observation point and pushes towards the plane, while less and less lies in front of it and pushes back. At the head all the charge is behind, and beyond it the field can only decrease, so our 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 always has a sharp maximum at the head (2.69 cm in case 5). The paper's maximum is rounded and lies at 2.29 cm. A smooth curve drawn by hand through a few computed points rounds off a sharp maximum and moves it inwards, and Figure 3 of the paper is hand-drawn (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:repro_fig3_digitization"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Checking the Digitization 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:repro_fig3_digitization"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To exclude a reading error, Figure 3 of the paper was digitized a second time, independently, from the scanned page: the axes were calibrated from the tick marks and the solid 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 curve was traced automatically. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:repro_fig3_digitization"
plural "false"
caps "true"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 shows the points of the first digitization (WebPlotDigitizer) on the scan, and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_fig3_digitization"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 compares the two readings of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
. They agree within about 0.5 kV/cm and both put the maximum at about 2.3 cm, much less than the differences of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:repro_fig3_reading"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
. The scan also shows that the figure is hand-drawn: the spacing of the tick marks on the vertical axis varies by up to 20%, and the curves are smooth where the computed field has a sharp corner.
\end_layout

