nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 show three things:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 keeps its shape and moves with the cloud, as in Figure 4 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
; the minima of our curves sit at the same places as the paper's (the apex and the drifted apex), and their depths are close. The RMS differences, 46 % before and 39 % after the drift, come from the width of the curves: the paper's curves are wider, the same difference found for Figure 3 in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:repro_fig3_cases"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, where it was shown that no choice of the charge constants removes it;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset
 stays attached to the rod and becomes weaker, because the cloud moves away from it;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the total field at the tip rises from 
\begin_inset Formula $-4.7\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $+40.8\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_fig4_tip"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Right after the burst the field at the tip is slightly reversed: the new cloud and its image in the rod more than cancel the applied field there, so no new streamer can start. A drift of less than a millimetre restores a strong field. This is the paper's conclusion: “a small drift toward the low voltage plane electrode can make a rapid increasing of the electric field in the surface of the rod tip”.
\end_layout

