\begin_layout Standard
The paper gives no crest voltage, so the crest is set to the 
\begin_inset Formula $50\,\%$
\end_inset
 breakdown voltage of the gap from the formula of Gallet et al. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gallet1975"
literal "false"

\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{p}=U_{50}=\frac{3400}{1+8/d}\;\text{kV}\qquad(d\text{ in m}),\label{eq:repro_gallet}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which gives 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}=161.9\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $d=40\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}=309.1\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $d=80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. The formula was fitted to gaps of about 1 m and longer, so for these gaps it is an extrapolation. The time to crest takes the values 15, 20, 30, 50, 75, 100, 150, 200, 300, 400, 500 and 600 µs, and each simulation stops at the second burst. The curves of the paper were read from the scanned figures, with the axes calibrated from the tick marks (the time axis is logarithmic), to within about 1 kV, 0.05 cm and 0.3 nC.
\end_layout

