\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Comparison with the paper's measured values (Comparison sheet): RMS of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:repro_nrmse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, the largest relative difference, and the change from the shortest to the longest front where the paper has values.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:repro_fig678_comparison"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
First streamer.
\series default
 Its inception voltage follows the measured values within 4 %, as it should, since they were used to fit the time-lag law. Its length (RMS 5 %) and charge (21 % and 10 %) are predictions: both decrease with the time to crest by about as much as the paper's (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_fig678_comparison"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). The charge is somewhat too high on the faster fronts of the 40 cm gap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Second streamer, inception.
\series default
 The same law, applied to the tip field that now includes the field of the first cloud, predicts the second inception voltage within 6 % (RMS 5 %), with no further fitting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Second streamer, length and charge.
\series default
 The length is right on the fast fronts but too long on the slow ones (2.32 cm against 1.66 cm at 600 µs), so it decreases less than the paper's (−31 % against −58 %). The charge is the weakest result (RMS 58 % and 66 %): ours is smaller than the first-streamer charge, while on fast fronts the paper's is larger (18.4 against 10.4 nC at 75 µs). The reason is the subtraction in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_cloud"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
: at the second burst the first cloud has drifted less than about a millimetre (0.1 mm at 15 µs, 1 mm at 600 µs), so almost all of it lies inside the new cone and is subtracted from the Gauss charge of the second streamer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Gaps.
\series default
 As in the paper, the charges are larger in the 80 cm gap (13.6 against 10.8 nC for the first streamer at 75 µs): the higher crest voltage gives a faster rise and, through the time lag, a later and stronger burst.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\series bold
Plane and dips.
\series default
 With 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:crossing_time"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 an ion needs about 7 ms to cross the 40 cm gap and 15 ms for the 80 cm gap, much longer than the impulses, so no cloud reaches the plane. No low-field dip had to be crossed in these simulations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary, with two pieces of the paper's data the model reproduces the inception voltages of both streamers and the length and charge of the first, including their decrease with the time to crest. The second-streamer length and, above all, its charge remain the open points (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_fig678_comparison"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
).
\end_layout

