\begin_layout Subsection
The Applied Field: Charge Simulation Method
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:csm"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The applied (geometric) field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{ext}$
\end_inset
 is the field of the charged electrodes when no space charge is present. It satisfies Laplace's equation with the rod at potential 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
 and the plane at zero potential. For a rod with a hemispherical tip there is no closed-form solution, so it is computed with the 
\series bold
Charge Simulation Method
\series default
 (CSM) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "singer1974,malik1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, which has been applied to the same hemispherically tipped rod-plane configuration in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "yildirim2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. As in the rest of this work, the origin is at the rod apex, the axis 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset
 points towards the plane, the centre of the hemispherical tip is at 
\begin_inset Formula $z=-R$
\end_inset
 and the grounded plane at 
\begin_inset Formula $z=d$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Idea of the Method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The surface charge of the rod is replaced by a set of 
\series bold
fictitious charges placed inside the rod
\series default
, where they cannot disturb the field in the gap. Their values are unknown. They are chosen so that the potential they produce is equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
 at selected points on the rod surface, called 
\series bold
contour points
\series default
. Once the charges are known, the field anywhere in the gap is the sum of their closed-form fields. The grounded plane is included with the method of images: every fictitious charge has a mirror charge of opposite sign beyond the plane, which makes the plane an equipotential at zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The placement of the fictitious charges and of the contour points, described step by step below, is shown in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:csm_tip"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/cms_tip_construction.pdf
	width 95col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Charge Simulation Method near the rod tip (drawn for 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=6$
\end_inset
): tip point charges 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset
 and shank line charges 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{j}$
\end_inset
 on the axis, contour points on the rod surface, and the construction of one tip charge from its angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset
.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:csm_tip"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout
\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 1: The Fictitious Charges (Unknowns)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All fictitious charges lie on the axis of symmetry, inside the rod:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Tip point charges.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset
 point charges 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\dots,N_{t}$
\end_inset
, inside the hemisphere, at
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{i}=-R+\frac{R}{2}\cos(\theta_{i})\;\left[\mathrm{m}\right],\qquad\theta_{i}=\left(i-\frac{1}{2}\right)\frac{\pi}{2N_{t}}\;\left[\mathrm{rad}\right],\label{eq:csm_tip_charges}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset
 is measured from the axis at the centre of the hemisphere (
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=0$
\end_inset
 at the apex, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\pi/2$
\end_inset
 where the hemisphere meets the cylindrical shank). Each charge lies half-way between the centre of the hemisphere and the height of its contour point (Step 3).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Shank line charges.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{s}$
\end_inset
 uniformly charged line segments with line densities 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{j}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\dots,N_{s}$
\end_inset
, along the axis of the shank, starting at the centre of the hemisphere and extending away from the plane. Segment 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset
 spans 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}\le z\le b_{j}$
\end_inset
 and has length 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell_{j}=b_{j}-a_{j}$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
b_{1}=-R,\qquad b_{j+1}=a_{j}=b_{j}-\ell_{j},\qquad\ell_{1}=\frac{\pi R}{N_{t}},\qquad\ell_{j+1}=g\,\ell_{j}\;\left[\mathrm{m}\right].\label{eq:csm_segments}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The segments grow by the factor 
\begin_inset Formula $g>1$
\end_inset
, so they are short near the tip, where the field changes quickly, and long far from it. Segments are added until their total length reaches the modelled rod length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{rod}\gg d$
\end_inset
, so that the far end of the rod does not influence the field near the tip.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unknowns are collected in one vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $N=N_{t}+N_{s}$
\end_inset
 elements:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{x}=\left[q_{1},\;\dots,\;q_{N_{t}},\;\lambda_{1},\;\dots,\;\lambda_{N_{s}}\right]^{T}\;\left[q_{i}:\mathrm{C},\;\lambda_{j}:\mathrm{C/m}\right].\label{eq:csm_unknowns}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 2: The Image Charges
\end_layout
\begin_layout Standard
The whole arrangement, with the images beyond the plane and the distances used for the field in Step 6, is shown in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:csm_images"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/cms_images.pdf
	width 95col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The fictitious charges of the rod and their images in the grounded plane (not to scale). Each image lies as far beyond the plane as its charge lies in front of it, at 
\begin_inset Formula $2d-z_{i}$
\end_inset
; 
\begin_inset Formula $P(z)$
\end_inset
 is an observation point on the axis.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:csm_images"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reflecting in the grounded plane 
\begin_inset Formula $z=d$
\end_inset
 maps a position 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $2d-z$
\end_inset
 and changes the sign of the charge:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the image of the point charge 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $-q_{i}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $2d-z_{i}$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the image of the segment 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{j}$
\end_inset
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[a_{j},b_{j}\right]$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $-\lambda_{j}$
\end_inset
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[2d-b_{j},\;2d-a_{j}\right]$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The images are not new unknowns: each one is tied to its real charge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 3: The Contour Points (Equations)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The potential is imposed at 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset
 contour points 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r_{k},z_{k}\right)$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,\dots,M$
\end_inset
, on the rod surface:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
on the hemisphere, one point per tip charge: 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r_{k},z_{k}\right)=\left(R\sin(\theta_{k}),\;-R+R\cos(\theta_{k})\right)$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,\dots,N_{t}$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
on the shank, three points per segment at a quarter, half and three quarters of its length: 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r_{k},z_{k}\right)=\left(R,\;a_{j}+f\,\ell_{j}\right)$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\in\left\{ \frac{1}{4},\frac{1}{2},\frac{3}{4}\right\}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This gives 
\begin_inset Formula $M=N_{t}+3N_{s}$
\end_inset
 equations, more than the 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset
 unknowns.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 4: The Potential Coefficients
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The potential of a point charge 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(0,z_{q}\right)$
\end_inset
, seen at a point 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r,z\right)$
\end_inset
, is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varphi=\frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{r^{2}+\left(z-z_{q}\right)^{2}}}\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right],\label{eq:csm_point_potential}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the potential of a uniform line charge 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset
 on the axis between 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $a<b$
\end_inset
) is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varphi=\frac{\lambda}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\ln\frac{F(b)}{F(a)}\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right],\qquad F(s)=\left(s-z\right)+\sqrt{\left(s-z\right)^{2}+r^{2}}\;\left[\mathrm{m}\right].\label{eq:csm_line_potential}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
potential coefficient
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{kn}$
\end_inset
 is the potential at contour point 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 produced by unknown 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 with unit value, together with its image. For a tip charge (
\begin_inset Formula $n=i$
\end_inset
):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{k,i}=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\left[\frac{1}{\sqrt{r_{k}^{2}+\left(z_{k}-z_{i}\right)^{2}}}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{r_{k}^{2}+\left(z_{k}-2d+z_{i}\right)^{2}}}\right]\;\left[\mathrm{V/C}\right],\label{eq:csm_P_point}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and for a shank segment (
\begin_inset Formula $n=N_{t}+j$
\end_inset
):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{k,N_{t}+j}=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\left[\ln\frac{F_{k}(b_{j})}{F_{k}(a_{j})}-\ln\frac{F_{k}(2d-a_{j})}{F_{k}(2d-b_{j})}\right]\;\left[\mathrm{V\cdot m/C}\right],\qquad F_{k}(s)=\left(s-z_{k}\right)+\sqrt{\left(s-z_{k}\right)^{2}+r_{k}^{2}}\;\left[\mathrm{m}\right].\label{eq:csm_P_line}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both cases the first term is the real charge and the second, with the minus sign, its image. The coefficients depend only on the geometry, not on the voltage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 5: The System of Equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By superposition, the potential at contour point 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 is the sum of the contributions of all unknowns, and it must equal the rod voltage:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^{N_{t}}P_{k,i}\,q_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{N_{s}}P_{k,N_{t}+j}\,\lambda_{j}=V\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right],\qquad k=1,\dots,M.\label{eq:csm_sum}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Written out for all contour points:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
P_{1,1}q_{1}+\dots+P_{1,N_{t}}q_{N_{t}}+P_{1,N_{t}+1}\lambda_{1}+\dots+P_{1,N}\lambda_{N_{s}}=V\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right]\\
P_{2,1}q_{1}+\dots+P_{2,N_{t}}q_{N_{t}}+P_{2,N_{t}+1}\lambda_{1}+\dots+P_{2,N}\lambda_{N_{s}}=V\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right]\\
\qquad\vdots\\
P_{M,1}q_{1}+\dots+P_{M,N_{t}}q_{N_{t}}+P_{M,N_{t}+1}\lambda_{1}+\dots+P_{M,N}\lambda_{N_{s}}=V\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right]
\end{cases}\label{eq:csm_system}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
or, in matrix form,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}P_{1,1} & \cdots & P_{1,N_{t}} & P_{1,N_{t}+1} & \cdots & P_{1,N}\\\vdots &  & \vdots & \vdots &  & \vdots\\P_{M,1} & \cdots & P_{M,N_{t}} & P_{M,N_{t}+1} & \cdots & P_{M,N}\end{bmatrix}}_{\substack{\mathbf{P}\;(M\times N)\\\left[\mathrm{V/C}\;\big|\;\mathrm{V\cdot m/C}\right]}}\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}q_{1}\\\vdots\\q_{N_{t}}\\\lambda_{1}\\\vdots\\\lambda_{N_{s}}\end{bmatrix}}_{\substack{\mathbf{x}\\\left[\mathrm{C}\;\big|\;\mathrm{C/m}\right]}}=V\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}1\\\vdots\\\vdots\\\vdots\\\vdots\\1\end{bmatrix}}_{\substack{\mathbf{1}\\\left[-\right]}}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\mathbf{P}\,\mathbf{x}=V\,\mathbf{1}\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right].\label{eq:csm_matrix}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{P}$
\end_inset
 belong to the unknowns (first the tip charges, then the shank segments) and its rows to the contour points (first the hemisphere, then the shank). Since 
\begin_inset Formula $M>N$
\end_inset
 the system is overdetermined, and it is solved in the least-squares sense: the charges minimize the deviation of the surface potential from 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
 over all contour points,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\min_{\mathbf{x}}\left\Vert \mathbf{P}\,\mathbf{x}-V\,\mathbf{1}\right\Vert ^{2}\;\left[\mathrm{V^{2}}\right]\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\mathbf{P}^{T}\mathbf{P}\,\mathbf{x}=V\,\mathbf{P}^{T}\mathbf{1}\;\left[\mathrm{V^{2}/C}\;\big|\;\mathrm{V^{2}\cdot m/C}\right].\label{eq:csm_least_squares}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times N$
\end_inset
 linear system is solved numerically. Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{P}$
\end_inset
 depends only on the geometry, the solution is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}=V\,\mathbf{x}_{1}$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{1}$
\end_inset
 is the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\,\text{V}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 6: The Applied Field on the Axis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the charges known, the field on the axis (
\begin_inset Formula $r=0$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $0\le z<d$
\end_inset
) follows from the closed-form fields of point and line charges. Every real charge lies below the observation point and every image above it, and all contributions point towards the plane:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}E_{ext}(z)=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\Bigg\{ & \sum_{i=1}^{N_{t}}q_{i}\Bigg[\underbrace{\frac{1}{\left(z-z_{i}\right)^{2}}}_{\text{point charge }q_{i}}+\underbrace{\frac{1}{\left(2d-z_{i}-z\right)^{2}}}_{\text{image }-q_{i}}\Bigg]\\
 & +\sum_{j=1}^{N_{s}}\lambda_{j}\Bigg[\underbrace{\frac{1}{z-b_{j}}-\frac{1}{z-a_{j}}}_{\text{line charge }\lambda_{j}}+\underbrace{\frac{1}{2d-b_{j}-z}-\frac{1}{2d-a_{j}-z}}_{\text{image }-\lambda_{j}}\Bigg]\Bigg\}\;\left[\mathrm{V/m}\right]\end{aligned}\label{eq:csm_field}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout
\begin_layout Standard
This is Coulomb's law in 
\series bold
discrete form
\series default
. For the space charge (Sections 2.3 to 2.5) the field is an integral of 
\begin_inset Formula $d\vec{E}=\frac{dq}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}r^{2}}\hat{r}$
\end_inset
 over a continuous charge distribution. Here there is a finite number of charges, so the integral becomes a sum: each point charge contributes 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{q_{i}}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}\,(z-z_{i})^{2}}$
\end_inset
, the inverse square of its distance from the observation point. For a line segment the integral of Coulomb's law along its length can be done in closed form, because 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{j}$
\end_inset
 is uniform:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int_{a_{j}}^{b_{j}}\frac{\lambda_{j}\,dz'}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}\left(z-z'\right)^{2}}=\frac{\lambda_{j}}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\left[\frac{1}{z-b_{j}}-\frac{1}{z-a_{j}}\right]\;\left[\mathrm{V/m}\right],\label{eq:csm_segment_integral}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which gives the brackets of the second sum; the image segments give the same expression with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{j}$
\end_inset
 replaced by their mirror positions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the charges are proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
, so is the field, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ext}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ext}(z)$
\end_inset
, which is used for the impulse in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:impulse_voltage"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Accuracy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The accuracy of the solution is checked by evaluating the potential at many points of the rod surface that were not used as contour points and comparing it with 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
. In this work 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=30$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $g=1.2$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{rod}=5d$
\end_inset
; with these values the surface potential deviates from 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
 by well below 
\begin_inset Formula $1\%$
\end_inset
, and increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset
 does not change the field near the tip.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rod length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{rod}$
\end_inset
 is part of the geometry of the test arrangement (Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:app_inputs"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). It is not a numerical setting: a longer rod spreads its charge over more length and slightly lowers the field near the tip. Rod lengths between 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $8\,\text{m}$
\end_inset
 change the field at the tip by about 
\begin_inset Formula $4\%$
\end_inset
 and the field at the head of the streamer by about 
\begin_inset Formula $2.5\%$
\end_inset
, which does not change any of the conclusions.
\end_layout

