\begin_layout Standard
The three conditions that define the waveform are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Crest position:
\series default
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{cr}$
\end_inset
 the slope of 
\begin_inset Formula $v(t)$
\end_inset
 is zero, 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{v}(T_{cr})=0$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Crest value:
\series default
 the voltage there equals 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $v(T_{cr})=1$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Half value:
\series default
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}$
\end_inset
 the voltage is 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}/2$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $v(T_{h})=1/2$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}>T_{cr}$
\end_inset
 (on the tail).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the first condition, the derivative of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:impulse_normalized"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 keeps the constant 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 in front, and setting it to zero at the crest gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\dot{v}(T_{cr})=k\left(-\alpha\,e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}+\beta\,e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\right)=0\;\Rightarrow\;-\alpha\,e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}+\beta\,e^{-\beta T_{cr}}=0,\label{eq:impulse_crest_condition}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
since 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq0$
\end_inset
 (otherwise 
\begin_inset Formula $v(t)=0$
\end_inset
 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
) and both sides can be divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
. Physically, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 only stretches the curve vertically: it sets how high the crest is, not when it occurs. The crest time therefore depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset
 alone, and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 is fixed by the second condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In equation form, with the unknowns 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
-\alpha\,e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}+\beta\,e^{-\beta T_{cr}}=0\\
k\left(e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}-e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\right)=1\\
k\left(e^{-\alpha T_{h}}-e^{-\beta T_{h}}\right)=\dfrac{1}{2}
\end{cases}\label{eq:impulse_system}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unknowns appear inside exponentials, so the system is non-linear and has no closed-form solution. In vector form, with the vector of unknowns 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}=\left[\alpha,\;\beta,\;k\right]^{T}$
\end_inset
, it reads
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{bmatrix}-\alpha\,e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}+\beta\,e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\\k\left(e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}-e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\right)-1\\k\left(e^{-\alpha T_{h}}-e^{-\beta T_{h}}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}.\label{eq:impulse_vector}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The system is solved numerically in Python (double_exponential_parameters in solver/utilities.py).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Time Lag of the Inception 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:fw_time_lag"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A streamer starts only after a free electron has appeared near the tip and its avalanche has grown, so the voltage at which it starts depends on how fast the voltage rises. Here this is described by a static threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}=V_{0}\,e_{ex}(0)$
\end_inset
 and an overvoltage that grows with the rate of rise of the tip field when it first reaches 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{tip}(t_{k})=E_{0}+k_{lag}\left(\left.\frac{dE_{tip}}{dt}\right|_{t_{k}^{*}}\right)^{n}.\label{eq:fw_time_lag}\tag{\ref{eq:burst_condition}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the first burst, with no space charge, this reads 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}=V_{0}+k\,(dV/dt)^{n}$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $k=k_{lag}\,e_{ex}(0)^{\,n-1}$
\end_inset
 in voltage units. The form follows from the volt-time area (disruptive effect) criterion introduced by Kind 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kind1957"
literal "false"

\end_inset
 and generalised later 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "darveniza1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
: inception occurs when
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int_{t^{*}}^{t_{inc}}\left[V(t)-V_{0}\right]^{m}dt=D,\label{eq:fw_disruptive_effect}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with constants 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset
. For a voltage that rises with slope 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset
 near 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
, the integral gives 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{m}\tau^{m+1}/(m+1)=D$
\end_inset
, so the delay is 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\propto S^{-m/(m+1)}$
\end_inset
 and the overvoltage 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}-V_{0}=S\tau\propto S^{1/(m+1)}$
\end_inset
, which is the law above with 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1/(m+1)$
\end_inset
. Kind's equal-area criterion, 
\begin_inset Formula $m=1$
\end_inset
, gives 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1/2$
\end_inset
. The constants are found once, from inception tests at several rates of rise (Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:app_inputs"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
), and the same law, written for the tip field, is used for every burst and every gap.
\end_layout


\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Inputs and Field Form
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The constants are measured in voltage form, as 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}=V_{0}+k\,(dV/dt)^{n}$
\end_inset
, in a test gap (Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:app_inputs"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). The program converts them once to field form with the applied field per volt at the tip of that gap, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ex}(0)$
\end_inset
 (Charge Simulation Method, Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:csm"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{0}=V_{0}\,e_{ex}(0),\qquad k_{lag}=k\,e_{ex}(0)^{\,1-n}.\label{eq:lag_field_form}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The field form is used in the simulation because what decides the inception is the field at the tip: it holds for any gap, and for every burst, where the tip field also contains the field of the older clouds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Numerical Evaluation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rate in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:fw_time_lag"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 is computed by the program itself, from the tip field it already knows at every instant,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{tip}(t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(0)+\sum_{j<k}E_{j}(0,t),\label{eq:lag_tip_field}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
While it integrates the drift of the clouds, the solver watches 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{tip}(t)-E_{0}$
\end_inset
. When this changes sign it locates the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}^{*}$
\end_inset
 by root finding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The rate is a backward difference over a short interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset
 (a thousandth of the current integration step, at least 
\begin_inset Formula $0.1\,\text{ns}$
\end_inset
):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\dot{E}_{tip}(t_{k}^{*})\approx\frac{E_{tip}(t_{k}^{*})-E_{tip}(t_{k}^{*}-\Delta t)}{\Delta t},\label{eq:lag_rate}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


where the positions of the clouds at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}^{*}-\Delta t$
\end_inset
 come from the interpolation of the drift solution. Before the first burst there are no clouds, and the rate is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ex}(0)\,dV/dt$
\end_inset
. A negative rate is set to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The required field 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{req,k}=E_{0}+k_{lag}\,\dot{E}_{tip}(t_{k}^{*})^{n}$
\end_inset
 is set, and the integration continues until 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{tip}(t)=E_{req,k}$
\end_inset
. That instant is 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}$
\end_inset
, and the burst is created (Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:bursts"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
).
\end_layout


