\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Applied impulse: \begin_inset Formula $V_{p}$
\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $T_{cr}$
\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $T_{h}$
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The impulse generator is set up for the required waveform, and the voltage on the rod is recorded for every shot with a calibrated voltage divider and a digital recorder, as specified in IEC 60060-2 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "iec60060_2"
literal "false"

\end_inset
. The crest value, the time to crest and the time to half value are read from the record as defined in IEC 60060-1 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "iec60060"
literal "false"

\end_inset
, where for switching impulses they are called the peak value, the time to peak 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{p}$
\end_inset
 and the time to half-value 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}$
\end_inset
; the standard switching impulse is 250/2500 µs. The double-exponential constants then follow as in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:impulse_fit"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Inception and time lag: \begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The three constants of the time-lag law (Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:fw_time_lag"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
) have the following meaning:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
 is the \series bold
static inception voltage
\series default
: the lowest voltage at which a streamer can start from the rod when the voltage rises very slowly. Below it the field at the tip is too weak for an avalanche to grow into a streamer. In field form, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}=V_{0}\,e_{ex}(0)$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 sets the \series bold
size of the overvoltage
\series default
: how far above 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
 the streamer starts for a unit rate of rise, in 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{kV}/(\text{kV}/\mu\text{s})^{n}$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 sets \series bold
how fast the overvoltage grows
\series default
 with the rate of rise; Kind's equal-area criterion gives 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1/2$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
They are found from inception tests:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\series bold
Static inception.
\series default
 A slowly rising direct or alternating voltage is applied and the voltage at which discharges first appear is recorded with a partial-discharge detector according to IEC 60270 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "iec60270"
literal "false"

\end_inset
. The test is repeated and averaged; this gives 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\series bold
Impulse inception.
\series default
 Impulses with several times to crest (for example 15 to 600 µs) are applied. In every shot the first streamer is detected from its current pulse, through a shunt in the rod or plane circuit, or from its light, with a photomultiplier, and recorded together with the voltage. The record gives the inception voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}=V(t_{1})$
\end_inset
 and the rate of rise 
\begin_inset Formula $dV/dt$
\end_inset
 at the instant the voltage passed 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\series bold
Statistics.
\series default
 Inception is a random event, so every front is repeated many times and the mean inception voltage is used, or the voltage of 50 % inception probability found with the up-and-down or multiple-level procedures of IEC 60060-1 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "iec60060"
literal "false"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\series bold
Fit.
\series default
 The law 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}=V_{0}+k\,(dV/dt)^{n}$
\end_inset
 is fitted by least squares to the pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(dV/dt,\,V_{1})$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
 from step 1 or as a third unknown.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The IEC standards specify the voltages, their measurement and the statistical procedures; the law itself and its fit follow Kind 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kind1957"
literal "false"

\end_inset
 and Darveniza 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "darveniza1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Cone angle \begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The corona burst is photographed in a darkened laboratory, with an open-shutter still camera or an image-converter camera triggered at the moment of the burst 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lesrenardieres1977"
literal "false"

\end_inset
. The first streamer appears as a bright fan of branched filaments that spreads from the rod tip. The cone angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 is the full opening angle of this fan, measured on the photograph between its two outer edges, with the apex at the tip; the length of the fan gives the streamer length, a useful check. Several shots are averaged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Stability field \begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
 is the lowest average field along which a positive streamer keeps propagating in air. It is a property of the gas, not of the electrodes, and it was found by measuring the voltage at which streamers just bridge a gap of known length, as the ratio of the two. In atmospheric air it is about 4.4 to 5 kV/cm 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "allen1995"
literal "false"

\end_inset
, and it increases with the air density and the humidity. It is therefore taken from the literature, for the conditions of the test, rather than measured again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Ion mobility \begin_inset Formula $\mu^{+}$
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The mobility of the positive ions left behind by the burst is measured in drift-tube experiments, from the time a swarm of ions needs to cross a known distance in a uniform field. It depends on the ion species and on the air density and humidity; for atmospheric air it is of the order of 1 to 2 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{cm}^{2}/(\text{V}\,\text{s})$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Shape constants \begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
 describe how the charge is distributed along the streamer zone, which depends on the branching and ionisation of its filaments. No laboratory measurement gives them directly: they are taken from the literature or chosen by the user, and can be checked against measured fields or charges. Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:app_bc"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 shows why they cannot be derived from the field condition of the model alone.
\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Time-Lag Parameters 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:app_time_lag"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The time-lag law of Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:fw_time_lag"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 has three constants, 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
. They were fitted to the measured first-streamer inception voltages of Figure 6 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $40\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 gap, 13 times to crest from 15 to 600 µs), with the script wh_time_lag_fit.py. For the first streamer there is no space charge yet, so the law reads
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{1}=V_{0}+k\left(\left.\frac{dV}{dt}\right|_{V=V_{0}}\right)^{n}.\label{eq:app_lag_voltage}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a trial set of constants, the impulse of each front time is built with the crest of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:repro_gallet"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 (161.9 kV) and a 2500 µs tail (Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:impulse_fit"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
); the instant at which it reaches 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset
 is found by root finding, its slope there gives 
\begin_inset Formula $dV/dt$
\end_inset
, and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_lag_voltage"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 predicts 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}$
\end_inset
. The constants minimise the sum of the squared relative differences from the measured values,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\min_{V_{0},k,n}\sum_{i=1}^{13}\left(\frac{V_{1}^{model}(T_{cr,i})}{V_{1,i}^{meas}}-1\right)^{2},\qquad V_{0}<\min_{i}V_{1,i}^{meas},
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
solved with a bounded least-squares method (scipy.optimize.least_squares). The result is listed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:app_lag"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 and shown in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:app_lag"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

