\begin_layout Subsection
Cone Angle from Figure 3 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:app_theta"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper does not give the cone angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
. For its Figure 3, however, it gives everything else about the first streamer: 
\begin_inset Formula $V=27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}=2.7\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $B=1.5$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $C=0.035\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}=5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $d=80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R=0.1\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. The head condition of the model, the total field at the head of the streamer equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
, is then one equation for the single unknown 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V\,e_{ex}(L_{cs})+E_{sp}(L_{cs};\theta_{B})+E_{I_{rod}}(L_{cs};\theta_{B})+E_{I_{plane}}(L_{cs};\theta_{B})=E_{c}.\label{eq:app_theta}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The angle enters twice: it sets the width of the cone in the field integrals, and through Gauss's law it sets the charge, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}=\pi\epsilon_{0}E_{c}L_{cs}^{2}\tan^{2}(\theta_{B}/2)$
\end_inset
, and with it the normalisation constant 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:amplitude_A"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
. For each trial angle the charge, the normalisation constant and the three space-charge fields at the head are recomputed, and a root-finding method adjusts the angle until 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:app_theta"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 holds (solve_theta_b in solver/theta_b.py, run by wh_fig3_reproduction.py). The result is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\theta_{B}=93.39^{\circ},\qquad Q_{cs}=11.41\,\text{nC},\qquad A=2.053\times10^{-6}\,\text{C}\,\text{m}^{B-3}.
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

