\begin_layout Standard
Three findings rule this approach out:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the optimum lies at the edge of the grid: a uniform field needs a charge spread almost evenly along the streamer, so the search pushes 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
 up and the density away from the concentration at the apex that the paper's form describes;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
many pairs score almost the same, so the field condition alone does not determine 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the calibrated constants do worse against the paper's own curves: for Figure 4 the RMS differences of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 are 57 % and 61 %, against 46 % and 39 % with the paper's values (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:repro_fig4"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
), and the predicted first streamer is shorter (2.0 cm against the paper's 2.7 cm).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
 describe how the charge is distributed inside the streamer zone, which depends on its branching and ionisation, physics this model does not contain. They are therefore inputs, given by the user; this work uses the paper's usual values.
\end_layout


